# Hook/CI parity baseline — gates CI runs that `.githooks/pre-push` deliberately
# does not, with the reason.
#
# Read by check_hook_parity() in .github/scripts/validate_plugins.py. One
# `key  # reason` per line. Anything CI invokes that the hook does not, and that is
# NOT listed here, is a hard CI failure.
#
# WHY A BASELINE AT ALL. Not every line in a CI `run:` block is a gate. CI has to
# provision its own environment; a developer's machine already has one, or resolves
# it differently. Without somewhere to record that, the only way to get the check
# green is to mute it — and a muted gate is worse than no gate, because it looks
# like coverage. (Same shape, and the same format, as `.release-parity-accepted`.)
#
# An ABSENT file means no omission is accepted. That is the strict reading, so
# deleting this file makes the check harsher rather than quieter — unlike
# `.leakdomains`, it needs no fail-closed guard of its own.
#
# Reasons here are what was VERIFIED, not what was plausible. An omission whose
# cause is unknown should say so: a confident wrong note is worse than an admitted
# gap, because the note is what the next person trusts instead of re-checking.

# --- environment provisioning, not a gate ---
# CI installs pytest into the runner before running the resume-gate suite. The hook
# reaches the same end by other means: it resolves an interpreter that already has
# pytest, or provisions a throwaway one with `uv run --with pytest`, and says loudly
# when it can do neither. So the DEPENDENCY is satisfied on both sides; only the
# mechanism differs, and there is nothing here for a path-keyed check to match.
-m pip  # CI provisions pytest; the hook resolves or provisions an interpreter that has it
